#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078220 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d17b0e14-aabc-5814-a242-d5fb803cb406" :question/math-academy-id "e-263" :question/problem "Given that the function\n![](images/f7/f72fd02a0488c44ac7472487222507c5739853cd25720502a726844e0c23c915.svg)\nis continuous at $x=0,$ find the value of the constant $b.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\sqrt{2b-3x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2b-3(0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(-x+2) \\\\  & =-(0)+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 2 & =\\sqrt{2b} \\\\ 4 & =2b \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078222 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/id #uuid "90e5fce3-26db-513d-9270-1e60b8dfb0d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-264" :question/problem "Find the value(s) of the constant $a$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n![](images/be/bee9ecfc2bcfaa7741ed1c2aca90b8a077c19715c2a642a9622856f986f434f3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2x+a)^{2} \\\\  & =(2(2)+a)^{2} \\\\  & =(4+a)^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ (4+a)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |a+4| & =1 \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ a & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ a & =-5,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=-5$ or $a=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078225 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b19955e9-3e5d-597e-81bc-f8f675e78e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-265" :question/problem "Given that the function\n![](images/47/47e2bbdd0741bbac490c3ac0851e95b2103078e22d06d4a369712236529e8302.svg) \nis continuous at $x=1$, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(ax+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(a(1)+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(a+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(2(1)+1) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(3) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(a+1) & =1 \\\\ a+1 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "aef91e7a-138c-58bc-a25c-ca77284581c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6279" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=4.$\n![](images/6e/6ed78555d5936e679b9aecd838d8b07f95031afe831eb3ef3e7cb64c5160f7c1.svg)" :question/worked-solution "At $x=4,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x) & =h(4) \\\\  & ={4}^{c(4)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4c+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(4x) \\\\  & =4(4) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{4c+1} & =16 \\\\ {4}^{4c+1} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 4c+1 & =2 \\\\ 4c & =1 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ for $c = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "687d7124-8f3c-5bea-b3ee-4893f23623d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506923} {:answer/id #uuid "806752b0-4ba0-5f93-b1f6-ea816f4e078b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506961} {:answer/id #uuid "8f968a81-57d7-5213-a960-ede6e6d41b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506997} {:answer/id #uuid "a14726ba-5859-5ee5-b523-53865b7f2801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507024} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7ead6f-afe9-59b3-9207-2eca8facc5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "687d7124-8f3c-5bea-b3ee-4893f23623d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506923} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff4a70a-fa81-5562-b828-116b69aaaafb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98316fee-a245-5ddf-ac9f-eeeebb819420" :question/math-academy-id "q-108026" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+1}, & x\\le 8 \\\\ ax+2, & x>8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=8,$ find the value of the constant $a.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=8,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}g(x) & =g(8) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(ax+2) \\\\  & =8a+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=8,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ 3 & =8a+2 \\\\ 8a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1751bc-d2e6-59da-9144-c493d8c0547c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506696} {:answer/id #uuid "219db4ab-5354-55af-bd09-10ec742a2080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506745} {:answer/id #uuid "49376de0-c212-5b80-a155-89abf421a3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506850} {:answer/id #uuid "82832309-4f9f-5257-ad6b-1044df232940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506966} {:answer/id #uuid "e35ddf70-d10a-5118-b952-23a76da2b572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "219db4ab-5354-55af-bd09-10ec742a2080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506745} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e11adba-b0ab-57f2-b48c-d273b76c0670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "791bb302-4274-58d7-bd6c-ad45a6412218" :question/math-academy-id "q-108030" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2x+1}, & x<4 \\\\ ax, & x\\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=4,$ find the value of the constant $a.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=4,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\sqrt{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x) & =g(4)=4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ 3 & =4a \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6040ff06-4666-5d8f-9449-f2d49d6f31af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506908} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab6c16c-0661-5ca3-93b4-5039f26ba532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506950} {:answer/id #uuid "868e5527-3381-5d70-8f99-9cdd03fac844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506974} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd6c3e4-e454-5321-b599-8ef7726a4569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506992} {:answer/id #uuid "8d27b3f9-a7e3-5dee-9206-cab3cbd4c212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6040ff06-4666-5d8f-9449-f2d49d6f31af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506908} :answer-field/id #uuid "dee07d2e-c0f5-5d98-9019-c91062364620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43cb36a7-f5a0-5d8b-b50f-29e51a864367" :question/math-academy-id "q-108125" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-b), & 2<x\\le 4 \\\\ 4-x, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at  $x=4,$ find the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=4,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x) & =g(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{2}-b) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-b),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(4-x) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-b) & =0 \\\\ 2-b & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24201f28-2359-5096-b10b-443d9c4e8bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506755} {:answer/id #uuid "2e92e377-da01-58c9-9022-5e4bc8867af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506792} {:answer/id #uuid "48966a52-4fb3-5071-a8ae-64994194a217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506848} {:answer/id #uuid "584cdb4e-95fa-5b82-8af0-99c2c5f4f031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506879} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e78c8d-143e-520e-9e4a-ec4103c37405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24201f28-2359-5096-b10b-443d9c4e8bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506755} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2cb3d3-1007-5698-8790-4bcaa328e707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9f66f38-e1cf-5b5b-b897-b0cb15d91b3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108126" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax), & 0<x\\le 3 \\\\ 2x-6, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at  $x=3,$ find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}g(x) & =g(3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x-6) \\\\  & =2(3)-6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3a) & =0 \\\\ 3a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da60575-04d6-58d3-8acb-e6760557e7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506821} {:answer/id #uuid "a09327ae-f217-5390-903b-8d1e415f3533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507022} {:answer/id #uuid "dabc081a-0d74-5f29-80e3-5c903ab46d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507151} {:answer/id #uuid "dffe4834-29fc-53bb-b011-f78f112ea933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507163} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa56fda-fb04-5c17-9f63-0a8133772f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dabc081a-0d74-5f29-80e3-5c903ab46d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507151} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb69bc17-560e-5c09-90bd-62ff9b0aa712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cc46709-7ae0-5a5e-9204-fad2911b8b44" :question/math-academy-id "q-108127" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+a), & -6\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ x-1, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at  $x=2,$ find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & =g(2)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(2+a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(x-1) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2+a) & =1 \\\\ 2+a & ={10}^{1} \\\\ 2+a & =10 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dca8ecee-5a2e-5987-b0bc-f1f6c6493d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dca8ecee-5a2e-5987-b0bc-f1f6c6493d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507157} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e1664b-4f08-532d-befc-cf96d8f41b95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc9cca92-ade4-5c5b-aec2-3c3abfe205eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-339925" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $b$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=5.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}bx+4, & x\\le 5 \\\\ 2x-1, & x>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "At $x=5,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(bx+4) \\\\  & =b(5)+4 \\\\  & =5b+4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(5)-1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=5,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 5b+4 & =9 \\\\ 5b & =5 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5$ for $b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa9cb21-34cc-5fff-bf58-732c2b371f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfa9cb21-34cc-5fff-bf58-732c2b371f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507130} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec5b3f7f-54f4-5da4-a769-a8d39d2bc7ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9482855-2b63-5790-9dcd-ba2963191a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-340142" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $b$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{bx}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{2}^{bx} \\\\  & ={2}^{b(2)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x) \\\\ {2}^{2b} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2b} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ 2b & =3 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdbed3c9-99d7-5f9c-bca0-9426b9978d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdbed3c9-99d7-5f9c-bca0-9426b9978d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507096} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bbbbc9e-ed3c-5ff6-8c0a-f4c17f9ec693" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d9d26f9-9e30-5030-aa28-1b26a93e7605" :question/math-academy-id "q-340151" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $b$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{bx}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{e}^{bx} \\\\  & ={e}^{b(1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) \\\\ {e}^{b} & =1 \\\\ {e}^{b} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ b & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b22328d-c40d-559f-8514-4063cdcfe2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b22328d-c40d-559f-8514-4063cdcfe2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506817} :answer-field/id #uuid "90485ac4-b636-5a69-95ba-60c0780560b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a1585fa-f5b4-5b2a-8fa5-be3318a05b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a1585fa-f5b4-5b2a-8fa5-be3318a05b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506929} :answer-field/id #uuid "faa1d67f-e3b7-5cb0-bf5f-4b09e8afe8c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6259a4b8-da5e-572e-ac26-51a1c1822402" :question/math-academy-id "q-340183" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}\\,(x+{b}^{2}), & x\\le 3 \\\\ x-1, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at  $x=3,$  find the possible values of $b.$\n\nArranged in **ascending order**,\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,b=$\n\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "At $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{2}\\,(x+{b}^{2})={log}_{2}\\,(3+{b}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(x-1)=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}\\,(3+{b}^{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3+{b}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 3+{b}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {b}^{2} & =1 \\\\ b & =-1,\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=3$ for the values\n\n$$\nb=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "498b4efd-e58f-5711-a9d4-2fe60c96df89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "498b4efd-e58f-5711-a9d4-2fe60c96df89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506852} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f741ac-06bc-5a16-a9a1-015dc79b23e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55ddc220-8d66-534a-9802-0f8e226d8a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340360" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-b}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 4x-2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=1,$ find the value of the constant $b.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\sqrt{x-b} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1-b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(4x-2) \\\\  & =4(1)-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{1-b} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{1-b})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 1-b & =4 \\\\ b & =1-4 \\\\ b & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8b9d11-e929-5904-a33a-d7331ded7086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506782} {:answer/id #uuid "3c71902d-3d6c-5967-8ad5-3ee84318ad2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506820} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4a0cf1-856f-5d93-b361-bdea86605d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507093} {:answer/id #uuid "d604faf9-467d-5021-b699-380019a9e7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507139} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ddcb8-d80e-5a85-b657-6535634901ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c71902d-3d6c-5967-8ad5-3ee84318ad2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506820} :answer-field/id #uuid "2228610a-ff86-59b6-85dd-63d22747e401" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc91a1b-1b4d-56a2-b2c1-01651ff7abbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37914" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $k$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k{x}^{2}+5x-2k, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 4x+3, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=-1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\  & =k{(-1)}^{2}+5(-1)-2k \\\\  & =k-5-2k \\\\  & =-k-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(4x+3) \\\\  & =4(-1)+3 \\\\  & =-4+3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ -k-5 & =-1 \\\\ -k & =4 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ for $k=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fdf315-e917-535a-a671-e4e9d1962c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506681} {:answer/id #uuid "13293f93-9f4b-53b2-91bb-d8dbbd8c3b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506717} {:answer/id #uuid "2cef4f52-b5b3-56a2-9b39-a19b8416682f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506784} {:answer/id #uuid "e294d13e-9f21-5646-b790-f1c497f849b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507172} {:answer/id #uuid "f5223efe-1754-52eb-a11c-9fa6dad58e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e294d13e-9f21-5646-b790-f1c497f849b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507172} :answer-field/id #uuid "82117fe7-6c3c-55d6-98af-41be9857182e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73018209-5cd9-51f0-b7f1-6d1df6445523" :question/math-academy-id "q-4590" :question/problem "Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b$ that make the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x=-2$ and $x=0.$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x, & x\\le -2 \\\\ a{x}^{2}+b, & -2<x<0 \\\\ 6, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=-2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}g(x) & =g(-2) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(a{x}^{2}+b) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{2}+b \\\\  & =4a+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}g(x) \\\\ 2 & =4a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+b) \\\\  & =a(0)^{2}+b \\\\  & =0+b \\\\  & =b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4a+b=2 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can replace $b$ by $6$ in the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+b & =2 \\\\ 4a+6 & =2 \\\\ 4a & =-4 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $x=0$ for $a=-1$ and $b=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c822341-4e56-50fd-ba09-fc792dcce56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{1}{2},\\,b=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506699} {:answer/id #uuid "240cb937-fa6f-5fbd-a8f5-c57634feafc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,b=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506754} {:answer/id #uuid "2b854fe0-ef1d-5321-90f8-585a9d8b0017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506780} {:answer/id #uuid "6055db78-569e-51e4-9439-86946d33caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506909} {:answer/id #uuid "82cdf392-a488-5d1c-b7fc-d8ed86588e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6055db78-569e-51e4-9439-86946d33caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506909} :answer-field/id #uuid "521ab5d3-f6d1-53a5-a68c-844d600c98bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a26cf28c-df8d-52da-88d5-cd7c098392bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-4594" :question/problem "Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-5, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2ax+3b, & -1<x\\le 2 \\\\ 4, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=-1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(-1)+3b \\\\  & =-2a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ -5 & =-2a+3b \\\\ 2a-3b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(2)+3b \\\\  & =4a+3b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 4a+3b & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-3b=5 \\\\ 4a+3b=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & =9 \\\\ a & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can replace $a$ by $\\frac{3}{2}$ into the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{3}{2})-3b & =5 \\\\ 3-3b & =5 \\\\ -3b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002f9002-0b3a-512a-a5b5-9c9c705ffcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506678} {:answer/id #uuid "11021f74-53a3-5d6a-920a-dd13858421a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506711} {:answer/id #uuid "265a260f-5985-56c7-a6d3-0cc3a6b34d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506764} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a41a26-f2c1-57c7-b0be-b73297cd197d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507051} {:answer/id #uuid "c804ede5-28de-5497-ba13-e5833f54bda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "002f9002-0b3a-512a-a5b5-9c9c705ffcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506678} :answer-field/id #uuid "5446e535-c33a-5234-a0f7-93f7a6051e75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e308fb0-dca1-532a-acd5-c75e34de5094" :question/math-academy-id "q-4602" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\sqrt{x}, & 0\\le x<4 \\\\ x+c, & x\\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=4,$ find the value of the constant $c.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=4,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(x+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =4+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\  & =4+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 6 & =4+c \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab7f9f1e-638e-53f0-b2a2-443a80df548d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507058} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a2407d-adc0-542d-9b1f-1a5243190226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507107} {:answer/id #uuid "e78a31fd-ea74-536f-88ca-63648fb7d643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507188} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f2c015-04a2-53c3-86d2-cf3b9d0f203b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507213} {:answer/id #uuid "fec11b82-635a-59b5-8a44-f7b592c82fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e78a31fd-ea74-536f-88ca-63648fb7d643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507188} :answer-field/id #uuid "81db2a0e-f85e-5dcb-9e4c-3054e9963864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "928df4f7-9920-5d89-a736-8ebd227a0dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48580" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $b$ that make the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2, & x\\le 1 \\\\ {b}^{2}+2xb, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =g(1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({b}^{2}+2xb) \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+2(1)b \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ -1 & ={b}^{2}+2b \\\\ {b}^{2}+2b+1 & =0 \\\\ (b+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(b+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |b+1| & =0 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$ for $b=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1590556-df58-5dfa-8cd2-a915d135a923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076851} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a16ad4-103b-57c4-97c3-fbe5192b77ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076852} {:answer/id #uuid "0611abe8-a8e4-56b6-87c4-9c42a04bffb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076853} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfdc5f4-7dd2-509f-b5bb-c17b7138326a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076854} {:answer/id #uuid "56267d71-84a7-5285-a7ad-16ada5b63ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56267d71-84a7-5285-a7ad-16ada5b63ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076855} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf456630-a37f-5980-9139-67494978b5c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79180d5f-62ff-58a4-b439-cf96cc9c56ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-48583" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{b-3x}+2, & x\\le 0 \\\\ x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=0$, find the value of the constant $b.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{b-3(0)}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{b}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(x+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{b}+2 & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{b} & =3-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283bc5fc-1222-57a7-8638-1198c9f5bbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506769} {:answer/id #uuid "2a76ca6d-751a-52d0-b413-74f07f4e9730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506776} {:answer/id #uuid "73a19e0a-5cd5-590e-bcf6-edf436ff926d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506944} {:answer/id #uuid "a78393aa-8611-5a23-aef6-c92572946ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507041} {:answer/id #uuid "c8abe998-8843-5f0b-99d1-f0f2263b0eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a78393aa-8611-5a23-aef6-c92572946ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507041} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6663233-d334-5d8d-952b-0dbf2517514c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eea34af5-c2e9-54d0-b79f-c71cb047b89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48621" :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(cx-4), & \\frac{2}{3}<x\\le 1 \\\\ 2x-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at $x=1,$ find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c(1)-4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(c-4) & =1 \\\\ c-4 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ c & =2+4 \\\\ c & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8205add4-3885-5e58-b68e-b319e4474a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076869} {:answer/id #uuid "917544cf-161d-5975-b64b-b472cebd4421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076870} {:answer/id #uuid "1c584d4c-cfae-5ffa-be0e-878a0906606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076871} {:answer/id #uuid "378649dc-10c5-573f-8c40-5f3d305b7d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076872} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddb90c9-e4c6-5493-80b0-e39376fb857e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "378649dc-10c5-573f-8c40-5f3d305b7d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076872} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a75b1ec-a1e1-5db1-8863-b757152306eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e7d303c-6761-5288-b0f2-9a9708133a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48646" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $a$ that makes the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2ax+1}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ 2x+5, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & =g(2) \\\\  & ={3}^{2a(2)+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{4a+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=2,$ the right-sided limit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+5) \\\\  & =2(2)+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4a+1} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{4a+1} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 4a+1 & =2 \\\\ 4a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a785d17e-bbf2-584f-9d3b-479d178e7329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507042} {:answer/id #uuid "cd91a7d5-47a3-53c2-baa1-472efdb61744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507125} {:answer/id #uuid "d015859d-11fe-5fa4-b0b7-922b686af8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507132} {:answer/id #uuid "e724d394-a0c1-5190-a170-1f9035a44af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507187} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f4e758-7704-5106-8489-813a8f687650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd91a7d5-47a3-53c2-baa1-472efdb61744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507125} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccd54b6a-abad-5f7c-9a8a-d0c1e669c070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8fcface-78f6-5f4f-81fc-4ada980d1046" :question/math-academy-id "q-76013" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{cx}\\,\\,, & x\\le 2 \\\\ {x}^{5}-5, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\  & ={3}^{c(2)} \\\\  & ={3}^{2c},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{5}-5) \\\\  & ={2}^{5}-5 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2c} & =27 \\\\ {3}^{2c} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ 2c & =3 \\\\ c & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $c=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8f785e-fd5a-5c93-bc9c-de10332326fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506789} {:answer/id #uuid "54aaa856-70a5-5a2b-bb57-b00c1b5f429c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506874} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4e456a-05f9-5618-beef-4b25e1e45363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506890} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9c0288-ed58-5ea6-89b9-e27a0f91630e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506900} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb25c15-4973-5e51-b8f4-ec36bde8ce89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54aaa856-70a5-5a2b-bb57-b00c1b5f429c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506874} :answer-field/id #uuid "894474d8-e79c-568b-8592-acf679f1a593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81da8b9c-598f-5273-b4fb-93dfa4451ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76014" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{4}^{cx}\\,\\,, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 6x+10, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & =h(1) \\\\  & ={4}^{c(1)} \\\\  & ={4}^{c},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(6x+10) \\\\  & =6(1)+10 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{c} & =16 \\\\ {4}^{c} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$ for $c=2.$"}]}
